      "id": "fe-bins-for-a-power-curve",
      "moduleId": "data-processing-features",
      "title": "Bins for a Wind Turbine's Output",
      "difficulty": "Easy",
      "minutes": 8,
      "overview": [
        "Nine readings from one wind turbine give the wind speed in meters per second and the power it produced in kilowatts:",
      ],
      "setup": [
        {
          "tex": "\\begin{array}{cccccccccc}\n\\text{speed (m/s)} & 3 & 15 & 5 & 9 & 26 & 4 & 11 & 7 & 18 \\\\ \\hline\n\\text{power (kW)} & 0 & 2000 & 90 & 700 & 0 & 40 & 1300 & 300 & 2000\n\\end{array}",
        },
        {
          "p": "Speed is cut into three bins, below 6, from 6 to 12.5, and 12.5 and above. Each bin becomes an indicator column, the first is dropped, and a linear regression with an intercept is fitted on the other two by least squares.",
        },
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "4.1",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "What power does the model predict at a wind speed of 20 m/s?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "With an intercept $b$ and weights $w_2, w_3$ on the two kept indicators, every row in the first bin is predicted $b$, every row in the second $b + w_2$ and every row in the third $b + w_3$. Three free numbers, one per bin, and the squared error of each bin depends only on its own number. A sum of squares $\\sum (y_i - c)^2$ is smallest at the mean of the $y_i$, so each bin is predicted its mean.",
            },
            {
              "p": "20 m/s falls in the third bin, whose training rows are 15, 18 and 26 m/s:",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\frac{2000 + 2000 + 0}{3} = 1333.3 \\text{ kW.}",
            },
            {
              "p": "For comparison the first two bins predict 43.3 and 766.7 kW.",
            },
            {
              "result": "1333.3",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "4.2",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "The 0 kW at 26 m/s is not a lull: turbines shut down above a cut-out speed, 25 m/s for this model, to protect the blades. You add a cut at 25, so there are four bins and three kept indicators. What does the model predict at 20 m/s now?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "The third bin now holds 15 and 18 m/s only, with mean $(2000 + 2000)/2 = 2000$ kW, and the 26 m/s reading has a bin of its own that predicts 0.",
            },
            {
              "p": "The first fit was not wrong arithmetic. It averaged a turbine at full power with a turbine that was switched off, because the cut points said nothing about how the machine works. Placing a cut where the physics changes is the kind of knowledge the model cannot find from nine rows, and it is what feature engineering adds. The price is the usual one for bins: the new bin rests on a single training row.",
            },
            {
              "result": "2000",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
