      "id": "llr-copied-column-hessian",
      "moduleId": "linear-logistic-regression",
      "title": "The Hessian with a Copied Column",
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "minutes": 5,
      "overview": [],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "mcq",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "A logistic regression's design matrix has three columns: ones, the soil temperature in degrees, and, through a merge mistake, the same temperature column again. The two classes overlap in temperature, so no line separates them. Which statement about its log loss $J$ is right?",
            },
            {
              "choices": [
                {
                  "text": "$J$ is no longer convex, so gradient descent can stop at a local minimum that is not the global one.",
                  "explain": "The copy cannot break convexity. For any X, v^T X^T S X v is a sum of S_ii (Xv)_i^2, which is never negative, so the Hessian stays positive semidefinite and J stays a bowl with no false bottoms. What the copy breaks is that the Hessian is invertible and the minimizer unique.",
                },
                {
                  "text": "$J$ is convex, its Hessian is singular at every $\\mathbf{w}$, and its minimizers form a line.",
                  "correct": true,
                  "explain": "With v = (0, 1, -1), Xv is the copy minus the original, a column of zeros, so X^T S X v = 0 at every w: the Hessian is singular everywhere. J is still convex, and flat along v, because moving weight from one copy to the other leaves every prediction unchanged. So J has a minimum value, reached on a whole line of weights with the same sum of the two temperature weights. Newton's solve fails at the first step, gradient descent from zero converges to the point of the line with the two weights equal, and a small ridge penalty restores a unique minimizer.",
                },
                {
                  "text": "$J$ has no minimum, because its Hessian is singular, as happens with separable data.",
                  "explain": "A singular Hessian is not the same as a loss with no minimum. Here the labels are mixed, so J does reach its smallest value, on a whole line of weights with the same sum of the two temperature weights. The weights run away only when the classes can be separated.",
                },
                {
                  "text": "$J$ has one minimizer, because $S$ has positive entries, which makes $X^\\top S X$ invertible.",
                  "explain": "Every S_ii = p_i(1 - p_i) is positive, but X^T S X can still be singular, because the zero comes from X: for v = (0, 1, -1), Xv = 0, so X^T S X v = 0 whatever S is. Positive weights on the examples cannot fix a design whose columns are dependent.",
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "With v = (0, 1, -1), Xv is the copy minus the original, a column of zeros, so X^T S X v = 0 at every w: the Hessian is singular everywhere. J is still convex, and flat along v, because moving weight from one copy to the other leaves every prediction unchanged. So J has a minimum value, reached on a whole line of weights with the same sum of the two temperature weights. Newton's solve fails at the first step, gradient descent from zero converges to the point of the line with the two weights equal, and a small ridge penalty restores a unique minimizer.",
            },
            {
              "result": "$J$ is convex, its Hessian is singular at every $\\mathbf{w}$, and its minimizers form a line.",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
