      "id": "lf-convex-in-the-logits",
      "moduleId": "loss-functions",
      "title": "Cross-Entropy Is a Bowl, Squared Error Is Not",
      "difficulty": "Hard",
      "minutes": 30,
      "overview": [
        "The lesson says that cross-entropy on a softmax is convex in the logits and that squared error on a softmax is not. This problem proves the first claim and finds a counterexample for the second.",
      ],
      "setup": [
        {
          "p": "Let $\\mathbf{p} = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf{z})$ for a vector of $K$ logits, and let $c$ be the true class. The loss is $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{z}) = -\\log p_c$, whose gradient is $\\mathbf{p} - \\mathbf{y}$ for the one-hot $\\mathbf{y}$. Moving logit $z_j$ changes $p_i$ at the rate $p_i(\\delta_{ij} - p_j)$, where $\\delta_{ij}$ is 1 when $i = j$ and 0 otherwise.",
        },
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "derivation",
          "requirement": "A complete derivation is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Show that the Hessian of $\\mathcal{L}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{z}$ is $H = D - \\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^\\top$, where $D$ is the diagonal matrix with $p_1, \\dots, p_K$ on its diagonal, and that $H$ does not depend on the true class.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Entry $i$ of the gradient is $p_i - y_i$, and $y_i$ does not move with the logits. Differentiating it with respect to $z_j$ gives",
            },
            {
              "tex": "H_{ij} = p_i\\,\\delta_{ij} - p_i p_j .",
            },
            {
              "p": "The first term is nonzero only on the diagonal, where it is $p_i$, which is the matrix $D$. The second is entry $(i, j)$ of $\\mathbf{p}\\mathbf{p}^\\top$. The label entered the gradient only through the constant $\\mathbf{y}$, so it drops out of the Hessian.",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "kind": "derivation",
          "requirement": "A complete derivation is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Show that $\\mathbf{v}^\\top H \\mathbf{v} \\ge 0$ for every $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^K$, so $\\mathcal{L}$ is convex. (Hint: read $\\mathbf{p}$ as a probability distribution over the $K$ entries of $\\mathbf{v}$.)",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "tex": "\\mathbf{v}^\\top H \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}^\\top D\\,\\mathbf{v} - (\\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})^2\n= \\sum_{i=1}^{K} p_i v_i^2 - (\\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})^2 .",
            },
            {
              "p": "Let $V$ be a random variable equal to $v_i$ with probability $p_i$. The sum is $\\mathbb{E}[V^2]$ and $\\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is $\\mathbb{E}[V]$, so",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\mathbf{v}^\\top H \\mathbf{v} = \\operatorname{Var}(V) \\ge 0 .",
            },
            {
              "p": "Every second directional derivative of the loss is therefore nonnegative, at every point, which is what convexity means for a twice differentiable function. The loss is a bowl in the logits, and gradient descent cannot get stuck in a local minimum that is not global. The variance is zero exactly when $V$ is constant, that is when $\\mathbf{v}$ is a multiple of $(1, \\dots, 1)$. Adding the same number to every logit leaves the softmax unchanged, so the bowl is flat in that one direction. $\\qquad\\blacksquare$",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.3",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Now take two classes written as one logit, $p = \\sigma(z)$, with label $y = 1$ and squared error $g(z) = (1 - \\sigma(z))^2$. A convex function satisfies $g\\big(\\tfrac{a + b}{2}\\big) \\le \\tfrac12\\big(g(a) + g(b)\\big)$. Compute $g(-2) - \\tfrac12\\big(g(-4) + g(0)\\big)$ to three decimal places. What does its sign say?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "With $\\sigma(-4) = 0.01799$, $\\sigma(-2) = 0.11920$ and $\\sigma(0) = 0.5$:",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\begin{aligned}\ng(-4) &= (1 - 0.01799)^2 = 0.96435, \\\\\ng(-2) &= (1 - 0.11920)^2 = 0.77580, \\\\\ng(0) &= 0.25 .\n\\end{aligned}",
            },
            {
              "p": "So $g(-2) - \\tfrac12(0.96435 + 0.25) = 0.77580 - 0.60718 = 0.169$. It is positive: the curve at the midpoint lies above the chord, which a convex function never allows, so squared error on a sigmoid is not convex in $z$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "For comparison, cross-entropy $\\log(1 + e^{-z})$ at the same three points gives $2.127$ at the midpoint against a chord value of $2.356$, below the chord as convexity requires.",
            },
            {
              "result": "0.169",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.4",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "The chord test shows the curve is not a bowl. Now see what that costs. An example with $y = 1$ starts at $z = -8$, and gradient descent with learning rate 1 updates only this logit: $z \\leftarrow z - g'(z)$. How many steps does it take to reach $z = -\\log 2 \\approx -0.693$, where $p = 1/3$, under squared error? An estimate within about 10% is enough.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "With $p = \\sigma(z)$ and $dp/dz = p(1 - p)$, the slope is $g'(z) = -2(1 - p) \\cdot p(1 - p) = -2p(1 - p)^2$, so each step raises $z$ by $2p(1 - p)^2$. Far below zero, $p \\approx e^{z}$ and $1 - p \\approx 1$, so the raise is about $2e^{z}$: $0.00067$ at $z = -8$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Treat the steps as a smooth flow, $dz/dt = 2e^{z}$. Then $e^{-z}\\,dz = 2\\,dt$, so $e^{-z}$ falls by 2 per step. It starts at $e^{8} = 2981$ and must fall to $e^{\\log 2} = 2$, which takes about",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\frac{2981 - 2}{2} \\approx 1490 \\text{ steps.}",
            },
            {
              "p": "Running the update exactly gives 1505 steps. The flow estimate is a little low because the true raise, $2e^{z} / (1 + e^{z})^3$, is smaller than $2e^{z}$ once $z$ nears zero. Almost all of the time goes on the first few units of $z$: the raise doubles only when $z$ has climbed by $\\log 2$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "This is the concave region at work. Differentiating the slope once more gives $g''(z) = -2p(1 - p)^2(1 - 3p)$, negative for every $p < 1/3$, that is for $z < -\\log 2$. The whole climb lies in that region, where the slope grows as $z$ rises, so it is smallest at the start, exactly where the example is most wrong.",
            },
            {
              "result": "1505",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.5",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Same start, same learning rate, but the loss is cross-entropy, $\\log(1 + e^{-z})$. How many steps does it take to reach $z = -\\log 2$?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "The cross-entropy slope is $p - 1$, so each step raises $z$ by $1 - p$. At $z = -8$ that is $0.9997$, and it stays close to 1 while $p$ is small. The logit climbs about one unit per step:",
            },
            {
              "tex": "-8 \\to -7.000 \\to -6.001 \\to -5.004 \\to -4.010 \\to \\dots",
            },
            {
              "p": "It needs to climb $8 - 0.693 = 7.3$ units, so 8 steps, against about 1,500 for squared error. The second derivative of cross-entropy, $p(1 - p)$, is positive everywhere, so there is no flat start to climb out of: the most wrong example gets the full step.",
            },
            {
              "result": "8",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
