      "title": "Five Classes, Two Schemes",
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "minutes": 10,
      "overview": [
        "A data set has 5 classes with 2,000 points in each. Training a binary SVM on $m$ points costs about $m^2$ operations, times a constant that is the same for every training run.",
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "How many times more work does one-vs-rest take than one-vs-one?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "One-vs-rest trains 5 classifiers, and each sees all 10,000 points: $5 \\times 10{,}000^2 = 5 \\times 10^8$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "One-vs-one trains $\\binom52 = 10$ classifiers, and each sees only the points of its two classes, 4,000: $10 \\times 4{,}000^2 = 1.6 \\times 10^8$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "The ratio is $5 \\times 10^8 / 1.6 \\times 10^8 = 3.125$. Twice as many classifiers, but each on 0.4 of the data, and the square makes the small ones cheap. Counting one-vs-one as 10 runs on all 10,000 points would make it look twice as expensive as one-vs-rest.",
            },
            {
              "result": "3.125",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "With $K$ classes of $n$ points each, and the same $m^2$ cost, what is the smallest $K$ for which one-vs-one is at least 10 times cheaper to train than one-vs-rest?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "One-vs-rest costs $K\\,(Kn)^2 = K^3 n^2$. One-vs-one costs $\\tfrac{K(K - 1)}{2}\\,(2n)^2 = 2K(K - 1)\\,n^2$. The ratio is",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\frac{K^3 n^2}{2K(K - 1)\\,n^2} = \\frac{K^2}{2(K - 1)},",
            },
            {
              "p": "which is $3.125$ at $K = 5$, as in Part 1, and does not depend on $n$. It is at least 10 when $K^2 \\ge 20(K - 1)$, that is $K^2 - 20K + 20 \\ge 0$. The roots of $K^2 - 20K + 20$ are $10 \\pm \\sqrt{80}$, about $1.06$ and $18.94$, so the smallest whole $K$ above them is $\\boxed{19}$. Check: $19^2 / 36 \\approx 10.03$, while $18^2 / 34 \\approx 9.53$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "The ratio grows about like $K/2$. The number of classifiers is the wrong thing to count. What matters is the total of $m^2$ over them.",
            },
            {
              "result": "19",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
