      "id": "ens-three-voters-one-test-set",
      "moduleId": "tree-based-models-ensembles",
      "title": "What Three Equal Models Can Vote",
      "difficulty": "Hard",
      "minutes": 20,
      "overview": [
        "Three classifiers of a yes-or-no label each get 13 of the same 20 test rows right, an accuracy of 0.65. A hard voting ensemble predicts the label that at least two of them predict.",
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "What is the highest accuracy the vote can have on these 20 rows?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Let $x_k$ be the number of rows that exactly $k$ of the three get right. Then",
            },
            {
              "tex": "x_0 + x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 20, \\qquad x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3 = 3 \\times 13 = 39 ,",
            },
            {
              "p": "and the vote is right on the $x_2 + x_3$ rows that two or three of them get right. Each such row uses at least two of the 39 correct answers, so $2(x_2 + x_3) \\le 39$ and $x_2 + x_3 \\le 19$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Nineteen is reached. Each model is wrong on 7 rows, 21 wrong answers in all, so let 19 rows take one wrong answer each and the 20th take two:",
            },
            {
              "list": [
                "model A is wrong on rows 1 to 6 and row 20,",
                "model B is wrong on rows 7 to 12 and row 20,",
                "model C is wrong on rows 13 to 19.",
              ],
            },
            {
              "p": "Each model is right on 13 rows. Rows 1 to 19 each have two right answers and row 20 has one, so the vote is right on 19 rows: accuracy $0.95$.",
            },
            {
              "result": "0.95",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "What is the lowest accuracy the vote can have?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Let $v = x_2 + x_3$ be the rows the vote gets right. A row the vote gets wrong holds at most one correct answer, and a row it gets right holds at most three, so",
            },
            {
              "tex": "39 \\le (20 - v) + 3v = 20 + 2v \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v \\ge 9.5 ,",
            },
            {
              "p": "and $v \\ge 10$. To get there, waste the correct answers: put them in rows where all three are right, which use one more than the vote needs, and in rows where only one is right, which lose the vote anyway.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Ten is reached: all three are right on rows 1 to 10, model A alone on rows 11 to 13, model B alone on rows 14 to 16, model C alone on rows 17 to 19, and none on row 20. Each model is right on $10 + 3 = 13$ rows. The vote is right on 10 rows: accuracy $0.5$, worse than any one of the three.",
            },
            {
              "result": "0.5",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.3",
          "kind": "explanation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "If the three models made their mistakes independently, each right with probability 0.65 on every row, the vote would be right with probability $3(0.65)^2(0.35) + 0.65^3 = 0.718$. Explain where that number falls between your two answers, and what the two extremes say about choosing the members of a voting ensemble.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "The vote ranges from 0.50 to 0.95 with the same three accuracies, so the accuracy of the members alone does not settle what the vote can do. What settles it is how their mistakes overlap.",
            },
            {
              "p": "The best case spreads the mistakes out so that no row is missed by two models: every mistake is outvoted. The worst case stacks them: the rows where one model is right are rows where the other two are wrong, so each correct answer is outvoted. Independent mistakes sit in between at 0.718, a gain of 0.07 over one model.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Models trained on the same data with similar methods tend to fail on the same hard rows, which pushes toward the worst case. A vote pays off when its members are about equally good and make different mistakes, for example a forest, a boosted model and a nearest-neighbor model, and it can lose to its best member when they share them. Before adding a member, check on validation data how often it is wrong on the rows the others get wrong.",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
