          "answer": [
            {
              "code": {
                "language": "python",
                "lines": [
                  "# forward: rows are examples, so the layer is X @ W1.T (not W1 @ X)",
                  "Z = X @ W1.T + b1          # (64, 32), b1 broadcasts across the batch",
                  "A = np.maximum(Z, 0)       # ReLU",
                  "",
                  "dA = rng.normal(size=(64, 32))   # an example incoming gradient, dL/dA",
                  "# backward, given dA = dL/dA of shape (64, 32)",
                  "dZ = dA * (Z > 0)          # ReLU derivative: 1 where Z > 0, else 0",
                  "dW1 = dZ.T @ X             # (32, 20), matching W1",
                  "db1 = dZ.sum(axis=0)       # (32,), summed over the batch",
                ],
              },
            },
            {
              "p": "The batch convention is the detail that trips people up. Part 2 wrote column vectors, giving $z = Wa$. With examples stored as *rows* the same computation is $Z = XW^\\top$, and the outer product $\\delta a^\\top$ becomes the matrix product $\\texttt{dZ.T @ X}$, which sums over the batch automatically. Checking shapes at every line is the fastest way to catch a misplaced transpose.",
            },
            {
              "p": "$\\texttt{(Z > 0)}$ is the ReLU derivative as a boolean mask, which NumPy promotes to $0/1$ on multiplication. Note it must be evaluated at the pre-activation $\\texttt{Z}$, not at $\\texttt{A}$. For ReLU the two masks always agree, since $\\texttt{A} = \\max(\\texttt{Z}, 0)$ is zero exactly where $\\texttt{Z} \\le 0$. The bias gradient sums over the batch axis because $b_1$ is added to every example, so it accumulates a contribution from each.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Whether $\\texttt{dW1}$ should be summed or averaged over the batch depends on whether your loss sums or averages over examples: the two differ by a factor of $1/64$, which silently rescales your effective learning rate if you get it wrong.",
            },
          ],
