      "id": "pca-components-for-noise",
      "moduleId": "principal-component-analysis",
      "title": "What a 95% Target Keeps",
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "minutes": 15,
      "overview": [
        "A dataset has 100 features, and its covariance matrix is the sum of two parts. The signal varies only inside a 5-dimensional subspace, with variance 8 along each of 5 orthonormal directions that span it. The noise has variance 0.6 in each of the 100 coordinates, independent across coordinates and of the signal. Treat the sample covariance matrix as equal to this one.",
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "What fraction of the total variance do the first five principal components explain?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Write the covariance as $\\Sigma = \\Sigma_s + 0.6\\,I$. If $\\Sigma_s \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}$, then $\\Sigma\\mathbf{v} = (\\lambda + 0.6)\\mathbf{v}$: adding $0.6\\,I$ keeps every eigenvector and raises every eigenvalue by 0.6. $\\Sigma_s$ has eigenvalue 8 on the five signal directions and 0 on the 95 directions orthogonal to them, so $\\Sigma$ has",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\underbrace{8.6, \\dots, 8.6}_{5}, \\;\\; \\underbrace{0.6, \\dots, 0.6}_{95} ,",
            },
            {
              "p": "with total $43 + 57 = 100$, which is also $5 \\times 8 + 100 \\times 0.6$, the trace. The first five explain $43/100 = 0.43$.",
            },
            {
              "result": "0.43",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is not required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "`PCA(n_components=0.95)` keeps the smallest number of components whose cumulative fraction of variance reaches 0.95. How many does it keep here?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "After the first five, each component adds $0.006$ to the cumulative fraction. Reaching $0.95$ needs $0.43 + 0.006\\,m \\ge 0.95$, so $m \\ge 86.7$ and $m = 87$. That is $5 + 87 = 92$ components: 91 reach $0.946$ and 92 reach $0.952$.",
            },
            {
              "result": "92",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.3",
          "kind": "derivation",
          "requirement": "A complete derivation is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Sketch the cumulative curve, say how many components you would keep, and say what reconstructing a row from that many components does to its noise.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "The curve climbs $0.086$ per component for five components, then turns into a straight line climbing $0.006$ per component up to 1. The bend at $k = 5$ is where the signal ends, and every component after it is noise.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Keep five. The reconstruction from them discards $57\\%$ of the variance, and all of it is noise: the 95 discarded directions carry $95 \\times 0.6 = 57$ of the 60 units of noise and none of the 40 units of signal. Projecting onto the five components removes all but 3 units of noise and keeps the signal whole, which is how PCA denoises.",
            },
            {
              "p": "The 95% target instead keeps 87 noise components, with $87 \\times 0.6 = 52.2$ units of noise in them, more than the signal's 40. A variance target treats noise variance as data to keep. With a finite sample the 95 noise eigenvalues spread around 0.6 instead of being equal (between about 0.52 and 0.68 for 20,000 rows), so the bend is slightly rounded, but it stays at five.",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
