          "requirement": "Reasoning is required. Coding is not needed.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Relate the eigenvalues of $C$ to the singular values of $X$, and explain why implementations compute the SVD of $X$ rather than eigendecomposing $C$.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Let $X = U\\Sigma V^\\top$. Then",
            },
            {
              "tex": "C = \\frac{1}{N}X^\\top X = \\frac{1}{N}V\\Sigma^2 V^\\top,",
            },
            {
              "p": "so the eigenvectors of $C$ are the right singular vectors $v_i$ of $X$, and the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_i = \\sigma_i^2/N$. The principal components are the columns of $V$, and the projections (scores) are $XV = U\\Sigma$.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Why the SVD is preferred: forming $X^\\top X$ squares the condition number, $\\kappa(X^\\top X) = \\kappa(X)^2$, exactly as in Part 7 of [Linear Regression: Normal Equations and Geometry](/theory/?id=theory-linear-regression-ols). Small singular values, precisely the trailing components, lose their precision entirely, and if $\\sigma_i$ falls below the square root of machine epsilon relative to $\\sigma_1$, the corresponding $\\lambda_i$ is numerically indistinguishable from zero. Rounding can even make it slightly negative, which is impossible for a true covariance eigenvalue and will break any code that takes a square root of it.",
            },
            {
              "p": "There is also a cost argument. Forming $C$ costs $O(Nd^2)$ and eigendecomposing it $O(d^3)$, which is prohibitive when $d \\gg N$: for $10{,}000$-pixel images with $500$ samples, $C$ is $10^8$ entries. A thin or truncated SVD of $X$ avoids ever materializing $C$, and randomized SVD extracts the top $r$ components in $O(Ndr)$.",
            },
          ],
