      "id": "pe-the-key-a-head-prefers",
      "moduleId": "relative-position-rope-positional-methods",
      "title": "The Key a Rotary Head Prefers",
      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "minutes": 12,
      "overview": [
        "A toy attention head works in two dimensions and uses RoPE with an angle of $\\theta = \\pi/10$ per position, chosen for clean numbers (the real ladder starts at 1 radian). Before rotation its query is $\\mathbf{q} = (1, 1)$ at every position and every key is $\\mathbf{k} = (-1, 1)$. The head is causal, so the query at position $m$ sees only keys at positions $n \\le m$. Write $r = m - n$ for how many positions back a key is.",
      ],
      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Which key does the head score highest? Give the smallest $r$ at which the score is largest.",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "Only the offset $n - m = -r$ matters, so the score is the query against the key turned by $-r\\theta$. With $\\varphi = r\\theta$, turning $(-1, 1)$ by $-\\varphi$ gives",
            },
            {
              "tex": "(-\\cos\\varphi + \\sin\\varphi,\\ \\ \\sin\\varphi + \\cos\\varphi),",
            },
            {
              "p": "and the dot product with $(1, 1)$ is",
            },
            {
              "tex": "\\text{score}(r) = 2\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{r\\pi}{10}\\right).",
            },
            {
              "p": "It rises from 0 at $r = 0$ through 0.618, 1.176, 1.618 and 1.902 to its maximum of 2 at $r = 5$, then falls again. The geometry says the same thing. The query points at 45 degrees and the key at 135 degrees, and turning the key back by 18 degrees per position lines it up with the query after $90 / 18 = 5$ positions. The maximum is $|\\mathbf{q}|\\,|\\mathbf{k}| = 2$, the largest a dot product of these lengths can be.",
            },
            {
              "p": "Using $R_{m-n}$ instead turns the key the other way, through 270 degrees, and puts the maximum at $r = 15$.",
            },
            {
              "result": "5",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "kind": "computation",
          "requirement": "Reasoning is required.",
          "prompt": [
            {
              "p": "Another key further back gets exactly the same top score. What is the next $r$ after the one from Part 1 with that score, and what does this say about a head with a single pair?",
            },
          ],
          "answer": [
            {
              "p": "The score $2\\sin(r\\pi/10)$ repeats every $2\\pi/\\theta = 20$ positions, so it is 2 again at $r = 25$, then at 45, 65 and so on. In the same way a key 20 back scores like the key at the query's own position.",
            },
            {
              "p": "With one pair the head cannot tell 5 back from 25 back. In real RoPE the pairs turn at speeds from 1 radian per position down to about $1/\\text{base}$, with periods that do not line up, so a key 25 back differs from a key 5 back on most pairs and a head can aim at one offset without the repeats.",
            },
            {
              "result": "25",
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
